BUILD IONIC BOND LAB 3D — AN INTERACTIVE VIRTUAL CHEMISTRY LABORATORY

Explore Electron Transfer, Ion Formation, and Ionic Crystal Structures

Create a complete, immersive, interactive educational website called IONIC BOND LAB 3D: Explore the World of Ionic Compounds for a high school chemistry presentation.

The website must use Indonesian as its primary language, with accurate scientific terminology appropriate for students in kelas X SMA.

MOST IMPORTANT DESIGN REQUIREMENT

This website must look and feel like a cute, modern, fully illustrated virtual chemistry laboratory, not a conventional educational website filled with text cards.

Students should feel as if they have entered a miniature chemistry laboratory where they can:

Explore interactive 3D atomic models.

Observe electron transfer between atoms.

Watch atoms form positive and negative ions.

Investigate the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.

Build and rotate 3D ionic crystal lattices.

Conduct virtual experiments with ionic compounds.

Discover the uses of ionic compounds in everyday life.

Complete interactive exercises and a chemistry challenge.

Prioritize these features in this order:

A visually rich virtual laboratory with recognizable laboratory equipment.

Interactive 3D atomic models showing electron shells and valence electrons.

An animated electron-transfer simulation with scientifically accurate ion formation.

Interactive 3D ionic crystal lattice models.

A virtual experiment investigating the properties of ionic compounds.

Real-world applications illustrated with relevant images.

Interactive exercises, explanations, and a 10-question quiz.

Do not create a website that merely displays chemical formulas, emojis, generic cards, or paragraphs. Every major learning section must contain meaningful scientific visuals and functional interactions.

Scientific principle: Ionic bonding is the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions. Electron transfer is a useful model for explaining how ions form, but ionic solids are extended three-dimensional lattices, not collections of separate NaCl or MgO molecules.

