Explanation panel

Display:

Element name and symbol.

Atomic number.

Electron configuration.

Number of valence electrons.

Typical ion formed in introductory ionic-bond examples.

Why the element may gain or lose electrons.

Examples:

Sodium tends to lose one electron to form Na⁺.

Chlorine tends to gain one electron to form Cl⁻.

Magnesium tends to lose two electrons to form Mg²⁺.

Oxygen tends to gain two electrons to form O²⁻.

Clarify that these are common ion-formation patterns, not a universal rule for every chemical environment.

5. ELECTRON TRANSFER STATION — WATCH ELECTRONS MOVE

Title: “Where Do the Electrons Go?”

This is one of the most important learning experiences.

Create an interactive electron-transfer simulator with animated atomic models.

Students must be able to select a metal and a nonmetal and observe a simplified electron-transfer process.

Required simulation A: Sodium chloride

Show a sodium atom beside a chlorine atom.

Initial electron distributions:

Na: 2, 8, 1.

Cl: 2, 8, 7.

When students press START ELECTRON TRANSFER:

Highlight the single valence electron of sodium.

Animate the electron moving from sodium to chlorine.

Update sodium to Na⁺ with electron distribution 2, 8.

Update chlorine to Cl⁻ with electron distribution 2, 8, 8.

Display the charges of the resulting ions.

Show the electrostatic attraction between Na⁺ and Cl⁻.

Display the formula NaCl.

Explain how the ions are arranged in an extended ionic lattice.

Reaction-style representation:

Na → Na⁺ + e⁻

Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻

Overall electron-transfer equation:

2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl

For the single-pair visualization, make it clear that the animation represents the simplified formation of an Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ion pair rather than the complete macroscopic reaction.

Required simulation B: Magnesium oxide

Show magnesium and oxygen atoms.

Initial electron distributions:

Mg: 2, 8, 2.

O: 2, 6.

Animate two electrons transferring from Mg to O.

Final states:

Mg²⁺: 2, 8.

O²⁻: 2, 8.

Display:

Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻

O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻

Overall equation:

2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO

Explain why the resulting ions have charges of +2 and −2 and combine in a 1:1 ratio in the empirical formula.

Required simulation C: Calcium chloride

Show one calcium atom and two chlorine atoms.

Animate the transfer of one electron to each chlorine atom.

Final ions:

Ca²⁺.

Two Cl⁻ ions.

Display the formula:

CaCl₂

Explain why one Ca²⁺ ion requires two Cl⁻ ions to produce an electrically neutral ionic compound.

Interaction controls

Provide:

SELECT ELEMENTS

START TRANSFER

PAUSE ANIMATION

CONTINUE

RESET

SHOW ELECTRON CONFIGURATION

EXPLAIN ION FORMATION

The animation must respond to user interaction.

Do not automatically play the entire sequence when the page loads.

Scientific explanation

Explain the difference between:

Losing electrons.

Gaining electrons.

Forming cations.

Forming anions.

Electrostatic attraction.

Forming an electrically neutral ionic compound.

Do not imply that atoms transfer electrons simply because they want to complete an octet. Present the octet rule as a useful introductory model and explain that the stability of the resulting compound depends on the overall energetics of the system.

6. IONIC BOND FORMATION STATION — BUILD THE CORRECT FORMULA

Title: “Build an Ionic Compound!”

Create an interactive workbench where students combine cations and anions to build electrically neutral ionic compounds.

Interactive ion library

Include:

Cations:

Na⁺.

K⁺.

Mg²⁺.

Ca²⁺.

Al³⁺.

Anions:

Cl⁻.

Br⁻.

F⁻.

O²⁻.

S²⁻.

Each ion should be represented by a clearly labeled 3D sphere or ionic model.

Use consistent colors, but also show each ion's name, symbol, and charge. Do not rely on color alone to communicate charge.

Build-a-compound activity

Allow students to drag or select ions and place them on a virtual balance.

The system should calculate the total positive and negative charge.

Example 1:

Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl

Total charge:

(+1) + (−1) = 0

Example 2:

Mg²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ → MgCl₂

Total charge:

(+2) + 2(−1) = 0

Example 3:

2Al³⁺ + 3O²⁻ → Al₂O₃

Total charge:

2(+3) + 3(−2) = 0

Example 4:

Ca²⁺ + O²⁻ → CaO

Total charge:

(+2) + (−2) = 0

Example 5:

2K⁺ + S²⁻ → K₂S

Total charge:

2(+1) + (−2) = 0

Functional requirements

When students add or remove an ion:

Update the number of selected ions.

Calculate the total positive charge.

Calculate the total negative charge.

Display the net charge.

Show whether the composition is electrically neutral.

Generate the correct simplest empirical formula when valid.

Explain how the ion ratio is determined.

Include a RESET COMPOSITION button.

Teach the criss-cross method as one possible shortcut, but emphasize that the fundamental principle is electrical neutrality and the simplest whole-number ratio.

Clarify that subscripts in an ionic formula represent the ratio of ions in the compound, not necessarily separate molecules.

