8. IONIC COMPOUNDS PROPERTIES STATION

Title: “How Do Ionic Compounds Behave?”

Create a visually rich experimental bench with interactive comparisons.

Divide the station into four investigations.

A. Melting point

Compare suitable ionic and molecular substances.

Use an illustrative comparison such as:

Sodium chloride: melting point approximately 801°C.

Magnesium oxide: melting point approximately 2,852°C.

Water: melting point approximately 0°C at standard atmospheric pressure.

Clearly label these as reference values and explain that different substances have different structures and intermolecular or electrostatic interactions.

Explain:

Ionic solids contain ions held in a three-dimensional lattice.

Considerable energy is generally needed to separate ions sufficiently for melting.

Ionic compounds can have high melting points, although values vary considerably between compounds.

Do not imply that every ionic compound has a higher melting point than every molecular substance.

B. Electrical conductivity

Show three interactive samples:

Solid sodium chloride.

Sodium chloride dissolved in water.

Molten sodium chloride.

When students select a sample, animate a conductivity tester.

Expected results:

Solid NaCl: does not conduct electricity effectively under ordinary conditions because its ions cannot move freely through the solid lattice.

Aqueous NaCl: conducts because mobile ions carry charge through the solution.

Molten NaCl: conducts because the ions are mobile in the liquid.

Use a lamp or meter animation to indicate the conductivity result.

Explain the difference between the presence of charged particles and their ability to move.

C. Solubility

Compare selected ionic compounds in water.

Include examples such as:

NaCl: soluble.

KNO₃: soluble.

AgCl: sparingly soluble.

CaCO₃: sparingly soluble.

Allow students to select a substance and observe an illustrative dissolution simulation.

Explain that solubility depends on the balance of energetic and entropic factors, including interactions between ions and water molecules.

Do not suggest that all ionic compounds dissolve readily in water.

D. Mechanical properties

Show a crystal model being subjected to a gentle conceptual force.

Animate a shift between neighboring layers of ions. When like-charged ions become adjacent, show their electrostatic repulsion and the crystal's tendency to fracture.

Explain why many ionic crystals are hard but brittle.

Label this as a conceptual illustration rather than a simulation of exact atomic-scale mechanics.

